<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Flint River (Georgia)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Flint River is a river of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, flowing south to join the Chattahoochee at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> border and form the Apalachicola. Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> towns lay along it in the 18th century, and the lands between it and the Ocmulgee were ceded under treaties of the early 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flint-river-georgia.jpg" alt="Flint River (Georgia)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Rivera · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oglethorpe_Ave_Bridge_over_Flint_River_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Flint River, historically also called the Thronateeska, runs 344 miles (554 km) and drains 8,460 square miles (21,900 km2) of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. It rises as ground seepage in East Point, in southern Fulton County on the edge of the Atlanta metropolitan area, and passes under the runways of Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta International Airport. It then flows south past Sprewell Bluff State Park and within 5 miles of Andersonville, through Albany, the largest city on its banks, to Lake Seminole near Bainbridge, where it meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chattahoochee-river">Chattahoochee River</a> above the Jim Woodruff Dam.</p><p>Geologists divide it into three sections: a deep channel cut into crystalline rocks in the red hills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, a broad forested swampy flood plain below the fall line near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-culloden">Culloden</a>, and a channel in limestone above the Upper Floridan Aquifer south of Lake Blackshear. With the Apalachicola and Chattahoochee it forms the ACF basin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the Civil War the Andersonville prison stood close to the river. The Flint has long been prone to floods, with significant ones in 1841 and 1925. In 1994 floods from Tropical Storm Alberto crested at 43 feet in Albany, forcing the evacuation of over 23,000 residents, inundating the town of Montezuma when the river topped its 29-foot levee, and lifting caskets from cemeteries. It was one of the worst natural disasters in the state's history, and Albany and Bainbridge flooded again in 1998.</p><p>In the 1970s a plan by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers to dam the river at Sprewell Bluff in Upson County was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-carter">Jimmy Carter</a>, then Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, whose hometown of Plains lies near the river, and other supporters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Flint is one of only 40 rivers in the nation to flow more than 200 miles without dams, and one of four in the southeast with significant populations of the Shoals spider-lily. It is impounded to form Lake Blackshear about 15 miles above Albany. Margaret Mitchell made it the border of the fictional plantation Tara in Gone With the Wind.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Flint River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The confederacy grew out of the survivors of Mississippian chiefdoms such as Coosa, along with refugees from other peoples, who regrouped in towns along the Coosa, Tallapoosa, Chattahoochee and <b>Flint</b> rivers in the 17th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/muscogee-confederacy">Muscogee Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
